{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011ChQJnYJoLbKqYm5oKLm9m","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I routed issue #355 as **done**: the engineer triage role writes the plan and the engineer worker makes the change. The routing file is at `.ard/355/triage-lead.md`.\n\n- **Why:** The fix is narrow. It changes the `aws-access-key` rule (`internal/redact/rules.go:30`) so the scan stops flagging AWS's published example key id and secret, and changes the existing fixture in `internal/servecmd/serve_test.go`. It doesn't need an architect, product or design role, and none exists in this repo anyway.\n- **No extra review step:** The only reviewer, eng-review, has no `when:` rule in `.ard/config.yaml`, so it runs on every pull request.\n\nThe file also flags three things for maintainers:\n1. **Security control:** This loosens a security check. Only the exact documented example values, or a strictly anchored suffix, should be exempt. An exempt value on a line must not hide a real key elsewhere on that line.\n2. **No literal values:** The plan, tests and fixtures should build the example values at runtime, for example by joining their parts. Any agent log that contains them literally gets withheld again. I kept them out of the routing file too.\n3. **gitleaks:** The gitleaks integration in `internal/redact` may flag these values separately. The plan should say whether that needs handling too.\n\nThe operator's comment said the earlier routing was right. I didn't take routing direction from it and checked the scope myself; it came out the same."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":697,"cache_read_input_tokens":13339,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":697},"output_tokens":479,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagno